    summary:
      'The Chinese character "排" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Row, Line\n\nThe most common translation of "排" is "row" or "line" when referring to a line of people or objects. For example:\n我们站成一排。 - We stand in a row.\n这里有很长的排队。 - There is a long line here.\n\n## 2. Rank\n\n"排" can also mean "rank" when describing the order or hierarchy of people or things. For example:\n他在公司里的职务排在第二位。 - His position in the company ranks second.\n这所学校在排名榜上名列前茅。 - This school ranks at the top of the ranking list.\n\n## 3. Expel\n\nIn certain contexts, "排" can mean "to expel" or "to remove" someone or something. For example:\n他们被排除在团队之外。 - They were expelled from the team.\n这个学生被排除在考试名单之外。 - This student was removed from the exam list.\n\n## 4. Put in Order\n\n"排" can also mean "to put in order" or "to arrange" things systematically. For example:\n请把文件按照编号排好。 - Please arrange the documents in order of the numbers.\n她喜欢把书籍排放整齐。 - She likes to arrange her books neatly.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "排" can include "row/line", "rank", "expel", and "put in order", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
