    summary:
      'The Chinese character "寄" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Send, Mail\n\nThe most common translation of "寄" is "send" or "mail" when referring to sending something to someone. For example:\n我会寄给你一封信。 - I will send you a letter.\n他寄了一份礼物给他的朋友。 - He mailed a gift to his friend.\n\n## 2. Depend on\n\n"寄" can also mean "depend on" when describing reliance on someone or something. For example:\n我们不能只寄希望于运气。 - We cannot just rely on luck.\n你可以寄信赖我们的团队。 - You can depend on our team.\n\n## 3. Lodge, Stay\n\nIn certain contexts, "寄" can mean "lodge" or "stay" when referring to temporary accommodation. For example:\n他在旅馆寄宿了一个晚上。 - He stayed at a hotel for one night.\n学生们在寄宿家庭里度过了整个学期。 - The students stayed with host families for the entire semester.\n\n## 4. Entrust\n\nWhen used in the context of "entrust", "寄" can mean to give someone responsibility for something. For example:\n请寄予我你的信任。 - Please entrust me with your trust.\n他将照顾孩子的任务寄予了他的父母。 - He entrusted his parents with the task of taking care of the children.\n\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "寄" can include "send/mail", "depend on", "lodge/stay", and "entrust", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
