      'The Chinese character "离" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Leave, Depart\n\nOne common translation of "离" is "leave" or "depart" when referring to someone leaving a place or departing from a location. For example:\n他离开了家乡。 - He left his hometown.\n飞机马上就要离开了。 - The plane is about to depart.\n\n## 2. Away from\n\n"离" can also signify "away from" when indicating a distance or separation from a specific point or object. For example:\n火车站离这里很远。 - The train station is far away from here.\n请离我远点！ - Please stay away from me!\n\n## 3. Apart, Divorce\n\nIn certain contexts, "离" can mean "apart" or be used in the context of divorce. For example:\n他们俩已经离婚了。 - They have already divorced.\n这两个城市离得很近。 - These two cities are very close to each other.\n\n## 4. Part, Separate\n\nAnother translation for "离" is "part" or "separate" when indicating a division or separation between two things or people. For example:\n我们无法分离。 - We can\'t be separated.\n这个问题很难分离。 - It\'s hard to separate this issue.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "离" can include "leave/depart", "away from", "apart/divorce", and "part/separate", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
