      'The French word "creuser" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. To dig\n\nOne of the main meanings of "creuser" is "to dig", referring to the action of excavating or removing material from the ground. For example:\nIl faut creuser un trou pour planter l\'arbre. - We need to dig a hole to plant the tree.\nLes archéologues ont creusé pour trouver des artefacts anciens. - The archaeologists dug to find ancient artifacts.\n\n## 2. To hollow out\n\n"Creuser" can also mean "to hollow out", indicating the action of making a hollow space inside an object. For example:\nElle a creusé une citrouille pour Halloween. - She hollowed out a pumpkin for Halloween.\nLes artisans ont creusé le bois pour en faire un vase. - The craftsmen hollowed out the wood to make a vase.\n\n## 3. To deepen\n\nIn certain contexts, "creuser" can be translated as "to deepen", suggesting the action of making something deeper. For example:\nLe fleuve a creusé un canyon au fil du temps. - The river deepened a canyon over time.\nIl faut creuser la discussion pour trouver des solutions. - We need to deepen the discussion to find solutions.\n\n## 4. To investigate\n\nWhen used metaphorically, "creuser" can mean "to investigate deeply" or "to delve into". For example:\nIl a creusé le sujet en faisant des recherches approfondies. - He investigated the topic by conducting in-depth research.\nLes journalistes ont creusé l\'affaire pour découvrir la vérité. - The journalists delved into the case to uncover the truth.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the French word "creuser" can include "to dig", "to hollow out", "to deepen", and "to investigate", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
