    summary:
      'The Chinese character "川" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. River\n\nThe most common translation of "川" is "river" when referring to a body of water flowing in a channel. For example:\n这条川流经整个村庄。 - This river flows through the entire village.\n黄河是中国最长的川。 - The Yellow River is the longest river in China.\n\n## 2. Plain\n\n"川" can also mean "plain" when describing a broad expanse of relatively flat land. For example:\n这片川十分肥沃。 - This plain is very fertile.\n大平原上有许多川地。 - There are many plains on the great plain.\n\n## 3. Prefix for Province Names\n\nIn certain contexts, "川" is used as a prefix for the names of certain Chinese provinces, such as Sichuan (四川 - Sìchuān). For example:\n我想去四川旅行。 - I want to travel to Sichuan.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "川" can include "river", "plain", and it is also used as a prefix for certain province names in China.',
