      'The Arabic word "مفهوم" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Concept\n\nThe most common translation of "مفهوم" is "concept" when referring to an idea or understanding. For example:\nلدي فكرة مفهومة عن الموضوع. - I have a clear concept/idea about the topic.\nهل فهمت المفهوم العام للخطة؟ - Did you understand the general concept of the plan?\n\n## 2. Comprehensible\n\n"مفهوم" can also mean "comprehensible" when describing something that is understandable or clear. For example:\nالشرح كان بسيطًا ومفهومًا. - The explanation was simple and comprehensible.\nاللغة الواضحة هي اللغة المفهومة. - Clear language is comprehensible language.\n\n## 3. Understood\n\nIn certain contexts, "مفهوم" can mean "understood" when referring to something that has been grasped or comprehended. For example:\nهل تم الامتحان وهل كل شيء مفهوم؟ - Did you take the exam, and is everything understood?\nالدرس كان واضحًا ومفهومًا للجميع. - The lesson was clear and understood by everyone.\n\n## 4. Intelligible\n\nWhen used in a more formal or technical context, "مفهوم" can mean "intelligible" in English, indicating something that is logical or reasonable. For example:\nالمعلومات يجب أن تكون مفهومة لكل المستخدمين. - The information should be intelligible to all users.\nتعليم الرياضيات يتطلب رؤية مفهومة للموضوع. - Teaching mathematics requires an intelligible view of the subject.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Arabic word "مفهوم" can include "concept", "comprehensible", "understood", and "intelligible", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
