    summary:
      'The French word "creuser" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Dig\n\nThe most common translation of "creuser" is "dig" when referring to excavating or creating a hole or trench. For example:\nIl a creusé un trou dans le jardin. - He dug a hole in the garden.\nNous devons creuser pour enterrer les câbles. - We need to dig to bury the cables.\n\n## 2. Hollow Out\n\n"Creuser" can also mean "hollow out" when describing the action of making an empty space inside an object. For example:\nElle a creusé une citrouille pour Halloween. - She hollowed out a pumpkin for Halloween.\nLes souris ont creusé des galeries dans le mur. - The mice have hollowed out tunnels in the wall.\n\n## 3. Explore, Investigate\n\nIn certain contexts, "creuser" can mean "to explore" or "to investigate deeply into a subject or issue". For example:\nIl faut creuser ce sujet pour en savoir plus. - We need to delve into this topic to learn more.\nLes journalistes ont creusé l\'affaire jusqu\'à trouver la vérité. - The journalists investigated the case until they found the truth.\n\n## 4. Worsen\n\nSometimes, "creuser" can also mean "to worsen" when describing a situation or problem becoming more severe. For example:\nLa crise économique a creusé les inégalités sociales. - The economic crisis worsened social inequalities.\nLes tensions politiques ont creusé le fossé entre les deux pays. - Political tensions have deepened the gap between the two countries.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the French word "creuser" can include "dig", "hollow out", "explore/investigate", and "worsen", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
