      'The Chinese character "至" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. To, Until\n\nThe most common translation of "至" is "to" or "until" when indicating a destination, endpoint, or limit in time or space. For example:\n我要走到教室至楼上。 - I need to go to the classroom upstairs.\n开放时间从上午九点至下午五点。 - Opening hours are from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.\n\n## 2. Arrive, Reach\n\n"至" can also mean "arrive" or "reach" when referring to reaching a certain point or level. For example:\n终于到达了终点至终点。 - Finally arrived at the finish line.\n公司的价值观是追求卓越，力争至顶峰。 - The company\'s values are to pursue excellence and strive to reach the top.\n\n## 3. Extreme, Ultimate\n\nIn certain contexts, "至" can be translated as "extreme" or "ultimate" to indicate the highest or most intense level. For example:\n他的要求高到了至难满足的地步。 - His demands are so extreme that they are nearly impossible to meet.\n这场比赛是至关重要的。 - This match is of ultimate importance.\n\n## 4. Even, Still\n\nSometimes "至" can convey the sense of "even" or "still" when emphasizing a point or situation. For example:\n他至今仍未放弃。 - He still hasn\'t given up.\n这个问题至专家都无法解决。 - Even the experts cannot solve this problem.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "至" can include "to/until", "arrive/reach", "extreme/ultimate", and "even/still", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
