      'The Italian term "gli" has a few different meanings depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Article\n\nIn Italian grammar, "gli" is an article that is used before masculine plural nouns that begin with a vowel, "z", "gn", or "s" followed by a consonant. For example:\ngli amici (the friends)\ngli uomini (the men)\n\n## 2. Pronoun\n\n"Gli" can also be a pronoun meaning "to him" or "to them" depending on the context. For example:\nHo dato il libro agli studenti. Gli ho dato il libro. (I gave the book to the students. I gave it to them.)\n\n## 3. Adjective\n\nIn some cases, "gli" can also be used as an adjective meaning "those" before masculine plural nouns. For example:\nGli studenti italiani (Those Italian students)\n\nThese are the main ways "gli" is used in Italian, either as an article, pronoun, or adjective, depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
