    summary:
      'The Chinese character "捂" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Cover, Hold\n\nThe most common translation of "捂" is "to cover" or "to hold" when referring to actions like covering something with hands or holding something with hands. For example:\n她捂着嘴笑了起来。- She covered her mouth and started laughing.\n他捂着孩子的手走路。- He held the child\'s hand as they walked.\n\n## 2. Muffle\n\n"捂" can also mean "to muffle" when describing actions like covering something to keep it quiet or to conceal a sound. For example:\n他捂住嘴巴不让自己大声说话。- He covered his mouth to prevent himself from speaking loudly.\n她捂住手机以免被听到。- She muffled her phone to avoid being heard.\n\n## 3. Withstand\n\nIn certain contexts, "捂" can mean "to withstand" when referring to withstanding pressure or heat. For example:\n大家挤在一起，直到最后一个人也捂不住。- Everyone squeezed in together, until even the last person couldn\'t withstand it.\n他捂不住高温而中暑了。- He couldn\'t withstand the high temperature and got heatstroke.\n\n## 4. Hold back\n\nWhen used in a more figurative sense, "捂" can mean "to hold back" in terms of withholding information or emotions. For example:\n他捂着真相，不愿多说。- He held back the truth and didn\'t want to say more.\n她捂不住自己的悲伤，泪水夺眶而出。- She couldn\'t hold back her sadness, and tears flowed from her eyes.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "捂" can include "cover/hold", "muffle", "withstand", and "hold back", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
