    summary:
      'The Spanish word "cita" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n1. **Appointment**\n\n   The most common translation of "cita" is "appointment" when referring to a scheduled meeting or arrangement. For example:\n   - Tengo una cita con el médico mañana. - I have an appointment with the doctor tomorrow.\n   - ¿Tienes una cita de trabajo esta tarde? - Do you have a work appointment this afternoon?\n\n2. **Quote**\n\n   "Cita" can also mean "quote" when referring to a passage or statement taken from a text or speech. For example:\n   - Me gusta esta cita de Shakespeare. - I like this quote from Shakespeare.\n   - El artículo incluye varias citas de expertos en el tema. - The article includes several quotes from experts on the topic.\n\n3. **Date**\n\n   In some contexts, "cita" can also mean "date" when referring to a romantic or social appointment. For example:\n   - Juan le pidió una cita a María para ir al cine. - Juan asked María out on a date to the movies.\n   - Tuvieron una cita romántica en un restaurante elegante. - They had a romantic date at a fancy restaurant.\n\n4. **Reference**\n\n   "Cita" can also mean "reference" when referring to a specific mention or allusion to someone or something. For example:\n   - El autor hizo una cita a un estudio reciente. - The author made a reference to a recent study.\n   - La cita a esta fuente es indispensable para el trabajo. - The reference to this source is essential for the work.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Spanish word "cita" can include "appointment", "quote", "date", and "reference", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
