      'The Chinese character "遗" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Inherit, Leave Behind\n\nThe most common translation of "遗" is "inherit" or "leave behind" when referring to passing down something or leaving something behind. For example:\n他遗传了父母的特点。 - He inherited his parents\' traits.\n这位艺术家遗留了许多珍贵的作品。 - This artist left behind many valuable works.\n\n## 2. Remember, Miss\n\n"遗" can also mean "remember" or "miss" when expressing nostalgia or longing for someone or something. For example:\n我们永远不会忘记遗憾的过去。 - We will never forget the regrets of the past.\n她深深地怀念着遗失的童年。 - She deeply misses her lost childhood.\n\n## 3. Remaining, Leftover\n\nIn certain contexts, "遗" can mean "remaining" or "leftover" when describing what is left after something. For example:\n剩下的食物全部遗留在桌子上。 - The remaining food was all left on the table.\n这是那场战役遗留下来的隐患。 - This is the hidden danger left over from that battle.\n\n## 4. Pass Away, Departed\n\n"遗" can also mean "pass away" or "departed" when referring to someone who has died. For example:\n他的父亲不幸遗世。 - His father unfortunately passed away.\n她的祖父母已经遗去多年。 - Her grandparents have departed for many years.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "遗" can include "inherit/leave behind", "remember/miss", "remaining/leftover", and "pass away/departed", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
