      'The Chinese character "垂" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Dangle, Hang Down\n\nThe most common translation of "垂" is "dangle" or "hang down" when referring to something suspended or drooping. For example:\n他的手臂垂在椅子上。 - His arm dangled on the chair.\n柳树枝上垂着垂穗。 - Willow branches droop with catkins.\n\n## 2. Flow\n\n"垂" can also mean "flow" when describing the movement of liquids or garments. For example:\n瀑布水垂直落下。 - The waterfall water flows straight down.\n水潺潺而过，织物垂在水中。 - The water flowed gently, with the fabric hanging in it.\n\n## 3. Respect, Admire\n\nIn certain contexts, "垂" can mean "respect" or "admire" when referring to feelings towards someone or something. For example:\n敬垂不已。 - Respect endlessly.\n垂帘听政。 - Rule from behind the curtain (a historical term for an emperor ruling indirectly).\n\n## 4. Bequeath, Pass Down\n\nWhen used as a verb, "垂" can mean "to bequeath" or "to pass down" something to the next generation. For example:\n垂恩后世。 - Pass down grace to future generations.\n他将这一技艺垂传给学生。 - He passed down this skill to his students.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "垂" can include "dangle/hang down", "flow", "respect/admire", and "bequeath/pass down", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
