    summary:
      'The Chinese character "概" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Outline, General\n\nThe most common translation of "概" is "outline" or "general" when referring to a summary or general understanding. For example:\n这是一个简要的概述。 - This is a brief outline.\n我对这个问题有个大概的了解。 - I have a general understanding of this issue.\n\n## 2. Approximately, Roughly\n\n"概" can also mean "approximately" or "roughly" when describing an estimation or approximation. For example:\n这里的人口大概有一百万。 - The population here is approximately one million.\n他的年龄大概在三十岁左右。 - His age is roughly around thirty years old.\n\n## 3. Principle, Concept\n\nIn certain contexts, "概" can represent "principle" or "concept" when referring to a fundamental idea. For example:\n这是他行事的原则之一。 - This is one of his guiding principles.\n我明白这个概念。 - I understand this concept.\n\n## 4. Sketch, Draft\n\nSometimes, "概" can mean "sketch" or "draft" when referring to a preliminary version or rough sketch. For example:\n他在几分钟内画出了一幅简笔概图。 - He sketched a simple draft in a few minutes.\n我们需要一个概览来开始。 - We need a sketch to start.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "概" can include "outline/general", "approximately/roughly", "principle/concept", and "sketch/draft", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
