    summary:
      'The Spanish word "pena" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Pain, Sorrow\n\nOne common translation of "pena" is "pain" or "sorrow" when referring to a feeling of sadness or distress. For example:\nSiento mucha pena por su pérdida. - I feel a lot of pain/sorrow for their loss.\nMe causa pena verlo sufrir de esa manera. - It saddens me to see him suffer like that.\n\n## 2. Penalty, Punishment\n\n"Penal" can also mean "penalty" or "punishment" in certain contexts. For example:\nEl árbitro señaló un penal en contra del equipo visitante. - The referee called a penalty against the visiting team.\nEl robo a mano armada lleva una pena de cárcel de varios años. - Armed robbery carries a prison sentence of several years.\n\n## 3. Shame, Embarrassment\n\n"Vergüenza" can be translated as "shame" or "embarrassment" in some situations where "pena" is used to convey a sense of embarrassment or discomfort. For example:\nMe da mucha pena tener que pedir ayuda. - It\'s very embarrassing for me to have to ask for help.\nNo pude evitar sentir pena ajena al ver su caída. - I couldn\'t help but feel secondhand embarrassment watching his fall.\n\n## 4. Worthwhile, Effort\n\nIn certain expressions or phrases, "pena" can also be translated as "worthwhile" or "effort" to convey that something is worth the trouble or effort. For example:\nVale la pena visitar ese museo. - It\'s worth visiting that museum.\nEl viaje merece la pena por las vistas que ofrece. - The trip is worthwhile for the views it offers.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Spanish word "pena" can include "pain/sorrow", "penalty/punishment", "shame/embarrassment", and "worthwhile/effort", depending on the specific context in which it is is used.',
