      'The Chinese character "麻" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Numb, Tingling\n\n"麻" can mean "numb" or "tingling" when describing a physical sensation. For example:\n我的手被刺麻了。 - My hand feels numb/tingling because it\'s asleep.\n\n## 2. Hemp\n\n"麻" can also refer to "hemp" or "flax" in English, particularly in relation to fabrics or materials. For example:\n这块布是用麻织的。 - This fabric is woven from hemp.\n\n## 3. Sesame\n\nIn some contexts, "麻" can mean "sesame" in English, often referring to the seed or oil. For example:\n麻酱 - Sesame paste\n麻油 - Sesame oil\n\n## 4. Complicated, Anxious\n\n"麻" can also convey a sense of "complicated" or "anxious" depending on the context. For example:\n这个问题很麻烦。 - This problem is very complicated/troublesome.\n他心情有些麻。 - He feels somewhat anxious.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "麻" can include "numb/tingling", "hemp/flax", "sesame", and "complicated/anxious", depending on how it is used in a sentence.',
