      'The Chinese character "占" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Occupy, Take Up\n\nThe most common translation of "占" is "occupy" or "take up" when referring to a space or location. For example:\n这张桌子占据了很多空间。- This table occupies a lot of space.\n这个城市的人口占全国的一半。- The population of this city accounts for half of the country.\n\n## 2. Fortune-telling, Divination\n\n"占" can also mean "fortune-telling" or "divination" when referring to predicting or determining future events. For example:\n她经常占卜未来。- She often engages in fortune-telling about the future.\n他向神占卜他的命运。- He consulted the gods to divine his fate.\n\n## 3. Seize, Make Use of\n\nIn certain contexts, "占" can mean "seize" or "make use of" when referring to taking advantage of a situation or opportunity. For example:\n他会占据这次机会。- He will seize this opportunity.\n我们应该占用这个资源。- We should make use of this resource.\n\n## 4. Proportion, Ratio\n\nWhen used in a mathematical or statistical context, "占" can mean "proportion" or "ratio". For example:\n男生占班级人数的三分之二。- Boys make up two-thirds of the class.\n这部分占总数的百分之五十。- This portion accounts for fifty percent of the total.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "占" can include "occupy/take up", "fortune-telling/divination", "seize/make use of", and "proportion/ratio", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
