    summary:
      'The Chinese character "怕" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Fear, Scared\n\nThe most common translation of "怕" is "fear" or "scared" when expressing a feeling of fear or being afraid. For example:\n我怕黑。 - I am afraid of the dark.\n他怕蜘蛛。 - He is scared of spiders.\n\n## 2. Fearful, Apprehensive \n\n"怕" can also convey a sense of being fearful or apprehensive about something in certain contexts. For example:\n她对考试很怕。 - She is fearful of exams.\n他对未来感到怕。 - He is apprehensive about the future.\n\n## 3. Avoid, Shun\n\nIn some cases, "怕" can mean "avoid" or "shun" when describing actions taken to stay away from something feared. For example:\n他怕遇到他的前任女友。 - He avoids running into his ex-girlfriend.\n她怕被批评所以不敢发表意见。 - She shuns expressing her opinions for fear of criticism.\n\n## 4. Danger, Risk\n\nWhen used in specific contexts, "怕" can also imply a sense of danger or risk. For example:\n他怕走夜路。 - He is afraid of walking on dark roads.\n她怕失去自己所珍视的东西。 - She fears losing something she treasures.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "怕" can include "fear/scared", "fearful/apprehensive", "avoid/shun", and "danger/risk", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
