      'The Chinese character "没" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Not, Have not\n\nThe most common translation of "没" is "not" or "have not" when used in negation or to indicate lack of something. For example:\n我没时间。 - I do not have time.\n他没去。 - He did not go.\n\n## 2. None\n\n"没" can also mean "none" when describing the absence of something. For example:\n这里没人。 - There is no one here.\n桌子上没东西。 - There is nothing on the table.\n\n## 3. Disappearance, Loss\n\nIn certain contexts, "没" can signify disappearance or loss of something. For example:\n车子没了。 - The car is gone/missing.\n钥匙没了。 - The keys are lost.\n\n## 4. Cancel, Dismiss\n\nWhen used in a formal setting or legal context, "没" can mean to cancel or dismiss. For example:\n会议被没了。 - The meeting was canceled.\n案件被没了。 - The case was dismissed.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "没" can include "not/have not", "none", "disappearance/loss", and "cancel/dismiss", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
