      'The Chinese character "穿" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Wear\n\nThe most common translation of "穿" is "wear" when referring to putting on clothes or accessories. For example:\n她喜欢穿漂亮的衣服。 - She likes to wear pretty clothes.\n他今天穿了一件新外套。 - He is wearing a new jacket today.\n\n## 2. Pierce, Penetrate\n\n"穿" can also mean "pierce" or "penetrate" when describing objects passing through something. For example:\n子弹穿过了窗户。 - The bullet pierced through the window.\n针穿透了纸张。 - The needle penetrated the paper.\n\n## 3. Through, Wear Through\n\nIn certain contexts, "穿" can be translated as "through" or "wear through" when describing wearing something until it becomes damaged. For example:\n我的袜子穿了。 - My socks have worn through.\n鞋子底部穿了一个洞。 - The bottom of the shoe has worn through with a hole.\n\n## 4. Thoroughly, Penetrate Deeply\n\nWhen used in some expressions, "穿" can mean "thoroughly" or "penetrate deeply". For example:\n他的目光穿透我的灵魂。 - His gaze penetrated deeply into my soul.\n战士穿透了敌方的防线。 - The soldiers penetrated deeply into the enemy\'s defense line.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "穿" can include "wear", "pierce/penetrate", "through/wear through", and "thoroughly/penetrate deeply", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
