    summary:
      'The Chinese character "声" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Sound\n\nThe most common translation of "声" is "sound" when referring to noises or voices. For example:\n这里的声音很大。 - The sound here is very loud.\n她的声音很好听。 - Her voice is very pleasant.\n\n## 2. Fame\n\n"声" can also mean "fame" or "reputation" when describing someone\'s renown or popularity. For example:\n他在学术界声名显赫。 - He enjoys a great reputation in the academic field.\n这家公司在业界拥有很好的声誉。 - This company has a good reputation in the industry.\n\n## 3. Shape\n\nIn a more literary or poetic context, "声" can refer to the shape or form of something. For example:\n鸟儿展翅而飞，留下一道美丽的声。 - The bird spreads its wings and leaves behind a beautiful shape.\n\n## 4. To make a sound\n\nWhen used as a verb, "声" can mean "to make a sound" or "to speak out". For example:\n她在房间里发出一声尖叫。 - She let out a sharp scream in the room.\n这位演员的表演让观众连连叫好，让人心生佩服之声。 - The actor\'s performance received continuous applause from the audience, evoking admiration.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "声" can include "sound", "fame/reputation", "shape", and "to make a sound/speak out", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
