    summary:
      'The Chinese character "翻" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Flip, Turn Over\n\nOne common translation of "翻" is "flip" or "turn over" when referring to physically turning something over. For example:\n他翻开书本。 - He flipped open the book.\n把煎饼翻过来。 - Flip the pancake over.\n\n## 2. Overturn\n\n"翻" can also mean "overturn" when describing a situation where something is reversed or changed dramatically. For example:\n这个判决被翻了。 - The verdict was overturned.\n他的生活被翻转了。 - His life was turned upside down.\n\n## 3. Translate\n\nIn certain contexts, "翻" can mean "translate" when referring to interpreting or converting languages. For example:\n我在翻译这本书。 - I am translating this book.\n\n## 4. Browse\n\nWhen used in informal contexts, "翻" can also mean "browse" or "flip through" when looking through a book, magazine, etc. For example:\n他翻阅了一本杂志。 - He flipped through a magazine.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "翻" can include "flip/turn over", "overturn", "translate", and "browse", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
