    summary:
      'The Chinese character "切" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Cut\n\nThe most common translation of "切" is "cut" when referring to actions like chopping or slicing. For example:\n请把水果切成小块。- Please cut the fruit into small pieces.\n他在切洋葱的时候切到了手。- He cut his hand while chopping the onion.\n\n## 2. Critical, Vital\n\n"切" can also mean "critical" or "vital" in certain contexts, emphasizing the importance of something. For example:\n这是一个非常重要、切不可忽视的问题。- This is a very important and critical issue that cannot be overlooked.\n他在公司中担任着一个切实关键的角色。- He plays a vital role in the company.\n\n## 3. Immediately\n\nIn some cases, "切" can convey a sense of immediacy or urgency. For example:\n这个问题需要切速解决。- This problem needs to be resolved immediately.\n学生们的安全是切须考虑的。- The safety of the students must be considered as a top priority.\n\n## 4. Thinly\n\nAnother possible translation of "切" is "thinly," often used in cooking instructions when slicing ingredients thinly. For example:\n把蔬菜切成片状。- Slice the vegetables thinly.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "切" can include "cut," "critical/vital," "immediately," and "thinly," depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
