      'The Chinese character "迎" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Welcome, Greet\n\nThe most common translation of "迎" is "welcome" or "greet" when referring to meeting or receiving someone. For example:\n迎接客人 - Welcome guests\n热情迎接 - Greet warmly\n\n## 2. Meet, Receive\n\n"迎" can also mean "meet" or "receive" in a broader sense of encountering or accepting someone or something. For example:\n迎头相对 - Meet head-on\n迎风而立 - Stand facing the wind\n\n## 3. To Face, To Confront\n\nIn certain contexts, "迎" can mean "to face" or "to confront" when describing direct encounters or opposition. For example:\n迎战敌人 - Face the enemy in battle\n迎难而上 - Confront difficulties bravely\n\n## 4. To Chase, To Pursue\n\nWhen used as a verb, "迎" can also mean "to chase" or "to pursue" in scenarios involving pursuit. For example:\n迎击敌军 - Chase and intercept the enemy forces\n迎头赶上 - Pursue and catch up\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "迎" can include "welcome/greet", "meet/receive", "to face/confront", and "to chase/pursue", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
