    summary:
      'The Chinese character "使" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Make, Cause\n\nThe most common translation of "使" is "make" or "cause" when it is used to indicate the action of making something happen or causing a certain outcome. For example:\n这个药使他感到更好。 - This medicine made him feel better.\n他的不专心使得他失去了比赛。 - His lack of focus caused him to lose the game.\n\n## 2. Send, Order\n\n"使" can also mean "send" or "order" when describing the act of sending someone to do something or giving an order. For example:\n他被使去参加会议。 - He was sent to attend the meeting.\n国王使他们进攻敌人。 - The king ordered them to attack the enemy.\n\n## 3. Let, Allow\n\nIn certain contexts, "使" can mean "let" or "allow" when indicating permission or enabling someone to do something. For example:\n父母使孩子去睡觉。 - Parents let their children go to sleep.\n这些规则使得我们能更好地合作。 - These rules allow us to cooperate better.\n\n## 4. Cause to Become\n\nAnother translation of "使" is "cause to become", indicating the action of causing someone or something to change or develop into a certain state. For example:\n这个项目使他成为一个专业的设计师。 - This project caused him to become a professional designer.\n这场风暴使得树木倒塌。 - The storm caused the trees to fall.\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "使" can include "make/cause", "send/order", "let/allow", and "cause to become", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
