    summary:
      'The Chinese character "是" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Is, Be, Yes\n\nThe most common translation of "是" is "is" or "be" when used as a verb to indicate existence or identity. It can also be translated as "yes" in certain contexts. For example:\n他是老师。 - He is a teacher.\n这是我的书。 - This is my book.\n你是对的。 - You are right.\n\n## 2. Correct \n\n"是" can also mean "correct" when used to affirm a statement or answer in agreement. For example:\n这个答案是对的。 - This answer is correct.\n你说的都是对的。 - Everything you said is correct.\n\n## 3. Indeed\n\nIn some cases, "是" can be translated as "indeed" to emphasize a statement or fact. For example:\n这个消息是真的。 - This news is indeed true.\n他是个好人，我知道。 - He is indeed a good person, I know.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "是" can include "is/be/yes", "correct", and "indeed", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
