    summary:
      'The Japanese character "こと" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Thing, Matter\n\nThe most common translation of "こと" is "thing" or "matter" when referring to a concept or idea. For example:\n重要なことを話したいです。 - I want to talk about an important matter.\nそのことを覚えている？ - Do you remember that thing?\n\n## 2. Event, Incident\n\n"こと" can also mean "event" or "incident" when describing a specific happening. For example:\n昨日のことを話そう。 - Let\'s talk about what happened yesterday.\nそのことで議論が起きています。 - There is a debate happening about that incident.\n\n## 3. Fact, Truth, Act\n\nIn certain contexts, "こと" can represent "fact", "truth", or "act". For example:\n彼女の言うことは本当です。 - What she says is true.\n良いことをした。 - I did a good deed.\n\n## 4. Verb Nominalizer\n\nWhen used in grammar, "こと" acts as a nominalizer for verbs, turning them into noun phrases. For example:\n歩くことが好きです。 - I like walking (the act of walking).\n話すことが大切です。 - Speaking is important.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Japanese character "こと" can include "thing/matter", "event/incident", "fact/truth/act", and it can also act as a verb nominalizer in certain contexts.',
