    summary:
      'The Chinese character "卧" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Lie Down\n\nThe most common translation of "卧" is "lie down" or "recline" when referring to the action of resting or sleeping. For example:\n他喜欢卧在沙发上看电视。 - He likes to lie down on the sofa and watch TV.\n卧室里有一张舒适的床。 - There is a comfortable bed in the bedroom.\n\n## 2. Crouching\n\n"卧" can also mean "crouching" when describing a posture or position where the body is close to the ground while bending the knees. For example:\n猫咪常常卧在地上观察周围。 - The cat often crouches on the ground to observe its surroundings.\n武术练习中，学生需要在卧的姿势下保持平衡。 - In martial arts practice, students need to maintain balance in a crouching position.\n\n## 3. Reclining\n\nIn certain contexts, "卧" can mean "reclining" or "lying in a relaxed position". For example:\n古代文人常常卧在竹榻上写诗。 - Ancient literati often reclined on bamboo couches to write poetry.\n温泉旅馆的客房内有舒适的卧榻供客人休息。 - The guest rooms in the hot spring hotel have comfortable reclining beds for guests to rest.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "卧" can include "lie down", "crouching", and "reclining", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
