    summary:
      'The Chinese character "实" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Real, True, Actual\n\n"实" can mean "real", "true", or "actual" when describing something that exists in reality. For example:\n这是一个实际问题。- This is a real/actual problem.\n他是一个实在的人。- He is a genuine person.\n\n## 2. Solid, Strong\n\nIn certain contexts, "实" can also mean "solid" or "strong" when describing physical qualities. For example:\n这块石头很实。- This stone is very solid.\n她是一个实力强大的选手。- She is a strong competitor.\n\n## 3. Practical, Realistic\n\n"实" can also mean "practical" or "realistic" when referring to something sensible or feasible. For example:\n这个计划很实。- This plan is very practical.\n他的想法很实在。- His ideas are very realistic.\n\n## 4. Truth, Reality\n\nWhen used in philosophical or abstract contexts, "实" can represent the concepts of "truth" or "reality". For example:\n追求真理和实相。- Pursue truth and reality.\n虚事实事。- Distinguish between the essential and non-essential matters.\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "实" can include "real/true/actual", "solid/strong", "practical/realistic", and "truth/reality", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
