      'The Chinese character "叨" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Mumble, Chatter\n\nThe most common translation of "叨" is "mumble" or "chatter" when referring to the sound of someone talking endlessly or incoherently. For example:\n他一直在叨叨个不停。 - He keeps mumbling nonstop.\n她总是喜欢叨叨些无关紧要的事情。 - She always likes to chatter about irrelevant things.\n\n## 2. Accompany\n\n"叨" can also mean "to accompany" when used in the sense of following someone or being by their side. For example:\n他喜欢有人叨着。 - He likes to have someone accompany him.\n孩子们经常有朋友叨着一起玩。 - The children often have friends accompanying them to play.\n\n## 3. Cling, Depend\n\nIn certain contexts, "叨" can mean "to cling" or "to depend" when describing someone relying on another person for support or help. For example:\n他总是叨着别人不放。 - He always clings to others and doesn\'t let go.\n她喜欢叨着家人的温暖。 - She likes to depend on the warmth of her family.\n\n## 4. Continually\n\nWhen used as an adverb, "叨" can mean "continually" or "repeatedly" to emphasize the continuous or repetitive nature of an action. For example:\n叨念着同一句话。 - Repeatedly nagging about the same thing.\n叨唠不停。 - Continually talking without a break.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "叨" can include "mumble/chatter", "accompany", "cling/depend", and "continually", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
