      '"Cascade" is a term used in various contexts such as computing, water features, and organizational structures. \n\n## 1. Computing\n\nIn computing, a "cascade" refers to a series of actions or events that are triggered by a single event. For example:\n- Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) use cascades to determine the final styles applied to an element based on multiple style rules.\n\n## 2. Water Features\n\nIn the context of water features, a "cascade" is a waterfall or series of small waterfalls. For example:\n- The garden had a beautiful cascade flowing into a pond.\n\n## 3. Organizational Structures\n\nIn organizational structures, a "cascade" can refer to the dissemination of information or directives down through different levels of an organization. For example:\n- The CEO\'s decision to restructure the company was cascaded down to all departments.\n\nSo, depending on the context, "cascade" can refer to a series of events in computing, a waterfall in nature, or the distribution of information in organizational settings.',
