    summary:
      'The Chinese character "赶" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Hurry, Rush\n\nOne common translation of "赶" is "hurry" or "rush" when describing quick or swift movements. For example:\n赶快走！- Hurry up and go!\n我赶着去上班。- I rushed to go to work.\n\n## 2. Overtake, Catch Up\n\n"赶" can also mean "overtake" or "catch up" when referring to catching up to someone or something. For example:\n我们要赶上最后一班地铁。- We need to catch the last subway train.\n他们终于赶上了领先的队伍。- They finally caught up with the leading team.\n\n## 3. Drive Away, Expel\n\nIn certain contexts, "赶" can mean "drive away" or "expel" when referring to forcing someone or something to leave. For example:\n赶走小偷。- Drive away the thief.\n他们被赶出了公园。- They were expelled from the park.\n\n## 4. Accompany, See Off\n\nSometimes "赶" can also mean "accompany" or "see off" when describing seeing someone off or accompanying them to a certain place. For example:\n赶着朋友去机场。- Accompanying a friend to the airport.\n他赶着奶奶去车站。- He escorted his grandmother to the train station.\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "赶" can include "hurry/rush", "overtake/catch up", "drive away/expel", and "accompany/see off", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
