    summary:
      'The Chinese character "涌" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Surging, Gushing\n\nThe most common translation of "涌" is "surging" or "gushing" when referring to water or emotions flowing rapidly. For example:\n河水涌动。 - The river water is surging.\n感情涌上心头。 - Emotions gush up in one\'s heart.\n\n## 2. Well Up\n\n"涌" can also mean "to well up" when describing something rising or emerging, especially emotions or tears. For example:\n泪水涌出眼眶。 - Tears well up from the eyes.\n感激之情涌上心头。 - Feelings of gratitude well up in one\'s heart.\n\n## 3. Surge, Rush\n\nIn certain contexts, "涌" can convey the idea of "surge" or "rush" related to a sudden movement or increase. For example:\n人潮涌动。 - The crowd surges.\n念头不断涌现。 - Thoughts keep rushing in.\n\n## 4. Boil Up\n\nWhen used metaphorically, "涌" can also mean "to boil up" in terms of feelings or thoughts becoming intense. For example:\n怒火涌动。 - Anger boils up.\n思绪涌现。 - Thoughts boil up.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "涌" can include "surging/gushing", "well up", "surge/rush", and "boil up", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
