      'The Chinese character "读" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Read\n\nThe most common translation of "读" is "read" when referring to the action of reading or literacy. For example:\n我喜欢读书。 - I like to read books.\n她在读报纸。 - She is reading the newspaper.\n\n## 2. Study\n\n"读" can also mean "study" when referring to the action of studying or learning. For example:\n他在读大学。 - He is studying in college.\n她很用功，一边读书，一边工作。 - She is very hardworking, studying and working at the same time.\n\n## 3. Pronounce, Read out loud\n\nSometimes "读" can also mean "pronounce" or "read out loud" when referring to the oral aspect of reading. For example:\n请读给我听。 - Please read it out loud for me.\n这个字怎么读？ - How do you pronounce/read this character?\n\n## 4. Look over\n\nIn certain contexts, "读" can mean "look over" or "examine" when referring to reviewing or going through something. For example:\n他正在读我的文章。 - He is looking over/examining my article.\n你有什么要我读的文件吗？ - Do you have any documents for me to look over/examine?\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "读" can include "read", "study", "pronounce/read out loud", and "look over", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
