    summary:
      'The Chinese character "正" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Positive, Correct\n\nOne common translation of "正" is "positive" or "correct" when referring to the direction, attitude, or correctness of something. For example:\n他对待问题总是很正面。 - He always has a positive attitude towards problems.\n这是一个正式的文件。 - This is a formal document.\n\n## 2. Just, Righteous\n\n"正" can also mean "just" or "righteous" when describing actions or behaviors. For example:\n做人要正直。 - One should be honest and righteous.\n法律要求我们做出正义的判决。 - The law requires us to make a just judgment.\n\n## 3. Straight\n\nIn certain contexts, "正" can mean "straight" when referring to lines, paths, or posture. For example:\n这条路很正。 - This road is very straight.\n他的姿势很正。 - His posture is very straight.\n\n## 4. Positive (Math)\n\n"正" is also commonly used in mathematical contexts where it is translated as "positive". For example:\n这个数字是正数。 - This number is positive.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "正" can include "positive/correct", "just/righteous", "straight", and "positive (math)", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
