    summary:
      'The Chinese character "趟" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Trip, Journey\n\nThe most common translation of "趟" is "trip" or "journey" when referring to traveling or a specific instance of travel. For example:\n我们这次趟的距离很远。 - The distance of our trip this time is very far.\n她这趟去北京出差。 - She is on a business trip to Beijing this time.\n\n## 2. Time\n\n"趟" can also mean "time" when counting instances of going or coming. For example:\n这是他们第二趟来这家餐厅了。 - This is their second time coming to this restaurant.\n我问了他两趟都没有回答。 - I asked him twice, but he didn\'t respond.\n\n## 3. Walk\n\nIn some contexts, "趟" can be translated as "walk", referring to a walk or stroll. For example:\n他每天晚上出去散步一趟。 - He goes for a walk every evening.\n我们一起去公园走了一趟。 - We took a walk in the park together.\n\n## 4. Wade, Wade through\n\n"趟" can also mean "to wade" or "to wade through", especially in the context of crossing a body of water. For example:\n他们趟过了河去对岸。 - They waded across the river to the other side.\n我们必须趟过这片泥地才能到达目的地。 - We have to wade through this muddy field to reach the destination.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "趟" can include "trip/journey", "time", "walk", and "wade/wade through", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
