      'The Chinese character "划" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Stroke, Draw\n\nOne common translation of "划" is "stroke" or "draw" when referring to actions like drawing lines, strokes, or marks. For example:\n她用笔划了一幅画。 - She drew a picture with a brush stroke.\n他划了一条直线。 - He drew a straight line.\n\n## 2. Row, Paddle\n\nAnother meaning of "划" is "row" or "paddle", commonly used when talking about rowing a boat or paddling. For example:\n他在湖上划船。 - He is rowing a boat on the lake.\n我们需要划船过去。 - We need to paddle over there.\n\n## 3. Plan, Scheme\n\nIn some contexts, "划" can also mean "plan" or "scheme" when referring to a strategic or calculated plan. For example:\n他们制定了一个划。 - They made a plan.\n这是一个好划。 - This is a good scheme.\n\n## 4. Check, Checkmark\n\nWhen used as a verb, "划" can mean "to check" or "to mark with a checkmark". For example:\n请划掉错误的部分。 - Please mark out the incorrect parts.\n她划了个勾表示同意。 - She checked with a tick to indicate agreement.\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "划" can include "stroke/draw", "row/paddle", "plan/scheme", and "check/checkmark", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
