      'The Chinese character "京" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Capital\n\nOne of the common translations of "京" is "capital", referring to a city that serves as the seat of government for a country or region. For example:\n北京 - Beijing (the capital of China) \n东京 - Tokyo (the capital of Japan)\n\n## 2. Scene, Scenery\n\n"京" can also mean "scene" or "scenery" when describing a picturesque view or landscape. For example:\n风景如画，美不胜收 - The scenery is picturesque and beautiful.\n京华烟云 - Scenic beauty of the capital\n\n## 3. Former\n\nIn certain contexts, "京" can mean "former" when describing something that used to hold a certain status or position. For example:\n京城 - Former capital\n京剧 - Peking opera\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "京" can include "capital", "scene/scenery", and "former", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
