    summary:
      'The Chinese character "行" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. To Walk, To Go\n\nOne common translation of "行" is "to walk" or "to go", referring to movement by foot. For example:\n我喜欢在公园里散步。 - I like to take a walk in the park.\n明天我们一起去商店行吗？ - Shall we go to the store together tomorrow?\n\n## 2. Line, Row\n\n"行" can also mean "line" or "row" when describing a formation or arrangement. For example:\n这些书摆放整齐，排成一行。 - These books are neatly arranged in a row.\n队伍整齐列成一行。 - The team is lined up neatly in a row.\n\n## 3. OK, All right\n\nIn informal contexts, "行" can be translated as "OK" or "all right", indicating agreement or approval. For example:\n你明天来吗？- Will you come tomorrow?\n行，我会的。 - OK, I will come.\n\n## 4. Profession, Business\n\n"行" can also refer to one\'s profession or business. For example:\n他在IT行业工作。 - He works in the IT industry.\n这是一家很大的家具行。 - This is a very large furniture business.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "行" can include "to walk/to go", "line/row", "OK/all right", and "profession/business", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
