    summary:
      'The Chinese character "揽" has different translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Embrace, Hug\n\nThe most common translation of "揽" is "embrace" or "hug" when referring to physically holding someone or something close. For example:\n他揽着她入怀。 - He embraced her in his arms.\n孩子们揽在一起互相安慰。 - The children hugged each other for comfort.\n\n## 2. Take on, Undertake\n\n"揽" can also mean "take on" or "undertake" when describing responsibilities or tasks. For example:\n她揽下了这项艰巨的任务。 - She took on this challenging task.\n公司揽了一些大单。 - The company undertook some big orders.\n\n## 3. Solicit, Seek\n\nIn certain contexts, "揽" can mean "solicit" or "seek" when referring to clients, customers, or opportunities. For example:\n他很擅长揽客。 - He is very good at soliciting customers.\n他们互相揽住了生意。 - They sought business from each other.\n\n## 4. Include\n\nAnother translation of "揽" can be "include" when talking about encompassing or covering something. For example:\n这份报告揽括了所有重要的细节。 - This report includes all the important details.\n这个计划应该揽盖更多的问题。 - This plan should cover more issues.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "揽" can include "embrace/hug", "take on/undertake", "solicit/seek", and "include", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
