    summary:
      'The Chinese character "争" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Fight, Struggle\n\nOne common translation of "争" is "fight" or "struggle" when describing a physical or metaphorical conflict. For example:\n他们在争夺冠军。 - They are fighting for the championship.\n这是一场激烈的争斗。 - This is an intense struggle.\n\n## 2. Compete, Contend\n\n"争" can also mean "compete" or "contend" when referring to competition or rivalry. For example:\n我们将会争取胜利。 - We will strive for victory.\n两队正在争夺第一名。 - The two teams are competing for first place.\n\n## 3. Seek, Vie for\n\nIn certain contexts, "争" can mean "seek" or "vie for" when describing efforts to obtain something. For example:\n他们争相购买这款新手机。 - They are vying to buy this new cell phone.\n各国争取在国际舞台上发声。 - Countries vie for a voice on the international stage.\n\n## 4. Argue\n\nWhen used as a verb, "争" can also mean "to argue" or "to dispute" in a verbal disagreement. For example:\n他们经常争吵。 - They often argue.\n这对夫妇总是为琐事争执不下。 - This couple always argue over trivial matters.\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "争" can include "fight/struggle", "compete/contend", "seek/vie for", and "argue", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
