    summary:
      'The Chinese character "举" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Lift, Raise\n\nThe most common translation of "举" is "lift" or "raise" when referring to physically moving something upwards. For example:\n他举起了一块巨石。 - He lifted/raised a huge rock.\n请举手发言。 - Please raise your hand to speak.\n\n## 2. Mention, Put forward\n\n"举" can also mean "mention" or "put forward" when referring to bringing up a topic or proposal. For example:\n他举出了一个很好的建议。 - He put forward a very good suggestion.\n这本书举例说明了这个概念。 - This book mentions examples to explain the concept.\n\n## 3. Carry out, Conduct\n\nIn certain contexts, "举" can mean "carry out" or "conduct" when referring to performing an action or activity. For example:\n举办会议 - to hold/conduct a meeting\n举行活动 - to carry out an event\n\n## 4. Support, Endorse\n\nSometimes "举" can be translated as "support" or "endorse" when referring to showing approval or backing for something. For example:\n全体成员举手同意。 - All members raise their hands to show approval/agreement.\n他被选举为我们的领袖。 - He was elected to lead us.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "举" can include "lift/raise", "mention/put forward", "carry out/conduct", and "support/endorse", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
