    summary:
      'The Chinese character "吃" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Eat\n\nThe most common translation of "吃" is "eat" when referring to consuming food or drinks. For example:\n我喜欢吃水果。 - I like to eat fruits.\n她每天吃三餐。 - She eats three meals a day.\n\n## 2. Consume\n\n"吃" can also mean "consume" in a broader sense, not just limited to food. For example:\n这个机器吃油很多。 - This machine consumes a lot of oil.\n他吃药的方式是每天早上吃一颗。 - His way of consuming medicine is to take one pill every morning.\n\n## 3. Suffer, Endure\n\nIn certain contexts, "吃" can mean "suffer" or "endure" when referring to hardships or difficulties. For example:\n他吃了很多苦。 - He has endured a lot of hardships.\n孩子们真能吃苦。 - The children can really endure hardships.\n\n## 4. Touch, Affect\n\nWhen used in specific situations, "吃" can mean "touch" or "affect" emotionally or physically. For example:\n这个故事很吃人。 - This story is very touching.\n他的言辞吃了她一个大亏。 - His words had a big impact on her.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "吃" can include "eat", "consume", "suffer/endure", and "touch/affect", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
