    summary:
      'The Chinese character "让" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Allow, Let\n\nThe most common translation of "让" is "allow" or "let", when indicating permission or giving someone the ability to do something. For example:\n父母让孩子自己决定。- Parents let children decide for themselves.\n老板让我休假一周。- The boss allowed me to take a week off.\n\n## 2. Yield, Give Way\n\n"让" can also mean "yield" or "give way" when referring to giving in or conceding to someone or something. For example:\n在交通中，要互相让让。- In traffic, we should yield to each other.\n他很固执，不愿让步。- He is very stubborn and unwilling to give in.\n\n## 3. Cause, Make\n\nIn certain contexts, "让" can indicate causing something to happen or making something occur. For example:\n这个问题让他很困惑。- This problem confuses him.\n这首歌让人感到快乐。- This song makes people feel happy.\n\n## 4. Others\n\nDepending on the specific context, "让" can also have other translations such as "surrender", "pass on", "yield", or "grant".\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "让" can include "allow/let", "yield/give way", "cause/make", and various other meanings, depending on the particular context in which it is used.',
