    summary:
      'The Chinese character "注" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Note, Annotation, Comment\n\nOne common translation of "注" is "note", "annotation", or "comment" when referring to written remarks or explanations. For example:\n请给这份文件加上注释。 - Please add comments/annotations to this document.\n我看了他的演讲并写了一些注。 - I watched his speech and made some notes/comments.\n\n## 2. Pour, Pouring\n\n"注" can also mean "pour" or "pouring" when describing the action of pouring liquid or substances. For example:\n请注一杯茶。 - Please pour a cup of tea.\n她注了一些水到花瓶里。 - She poured some water into the vase.\n\n## 3. Focus, Attention\n\nIn certain contexts, "注" can convey the idea of "focus" or "attention" when referring to concentration or emphasis. For example:\n他全神贯注地看着比赛。 - He watched the game with full attention/concentration.\n这次会议的重点应该注在这个问题上。 - The focus of this meeting should be on this issue.\n\n## 4. Inject, Injection\n\nAnother translation of "注" is "inject" or "injection" when related to medical procedures or the act of injecting a substance. For example:\n医生给他注射了一针。 - The doctor gave him an injection.\n肉类通常含有添加了调味料的注水。 - Meat often contains injected water with seasonings.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "注" can include "note/annotation/comment", "pour/pouring", "focus/attention", and "inject/injection", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
