    summary:
      'The Chinese character "诉" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Sue, Accuse\n\nOne common translation of "诉" is "sue" or "accuse", often used in legal contexts. For example:\n他准备起诉公司。 - He is preparing to sue the company.\n他被控告谋杀罪。 - He was accused of murder.\n\n## 2. Complain\n\n"诉" can also mean "to complain" or "to voice grievances". For example:\n她向老板诉苦。 - She complained to the boss.\n学生们听取了市民的诉求。 - The students listened to the citizens\' complaints.\n\n## 3. Petition\n\nIn certain contexts, "诉" can be translated as "petition" when referring to making a formal request. For example:\n他向政府提出了一份诉状。 - He submitted a petition to the government.\n群众向当地政府提诉愿。 - The masses petitioned the local government.\n\n## 4. Tell\n\nSometimes, "诉" can simply mean "to tell" or "to narrate". For example:\n他诉说了他的遭遇。 - He told/narrated his experiences.\n她诉以情感。 - She expressed her feelings.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "诉" can include "sue/accuse", "complain", "petition", and "tell", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
