      'The Chinese character "烟" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Smoke\n\nThe most common translation of "烟" is "smoke" when referring to the visible gaseous products of burning materials, often associated with fire or cigarettes. For example:\n房间里弥漫着浓烟。 - The room is filled with thick smoke.\n抽烟对健康有害。 - Smoking is harmful to health.\n\n## 2. Fog, Mist\n\n"烟" can also mean "fog" or "mist" when describing a cloud-like, opaque collection of tiny water droplets suspended in the atmosphere. For example:\n山顶被云雾笼罩，宛如仙境朦胧。 - The mountaintop is shrouded in mist, resembling a fairyland.\n\n## 3. Cigarette\n\nIn colloquial language, "烟" can also refer specifically to a "cigarette". For example:\n他在吸一支烟。 - He is smoking a cigarette.\n抽烟是一种不健康的习惯。 - Smoking is an unhealthy habit.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "烟" can include "smoke", "fog/mist", and "cigarette", depending on the specific context in which it is used.\n',
