    summary:
      'The Chinese character "相" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Mutual, each other\n\nOne of the meanings of "相" is "mutual" or "each other" when used in phrases indicating a mutual relationship between two or more parties. For example:\n他们互相帮助。 - They help each other.\n我们相互尊重。 - We respect each other.\n\n## 2. Appearance, image\n\n"相" can also mean "appearance" or "image" when describing the outward look or impression of someone or something. For example:\n他的相貌很英俊。 - His appearance is very handsome.\n这幅画的色彩很相协调。 - The colors in this painting are very harmonious.\n\n## 3. Match, correspond\n\nIn certain contexts, "相" can mean "match" or "correspond" when talking about things that complement or fit together. For example:\n他们的性格相合。 - Their personalities match.\n这两幅画相得益彰。 - These two paintings complement each other well.\n\n## 4. Minister, secretary\n\n"相" can also refer to a minister or secretary in historical or governmental contexts. For example:\n相国 - Prime Minister\n丞相 - Chancellor\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "相" can include "mutual/each other", "appearance/image", "match/correspond", and titles like "minister/secretary", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
