    summary:
      'The Chinese character "掉" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Drop, Fall\n\nThe most common translation of "掉" is "drop" or "fall" when describing something descending or coming off. For example:\n她掉了一枚耳环。 - She dropped a earring.\n雨水掉下来了。 - The rain is falling down.\n\n## 2. Lose\n\n"掉" can also mean "lose" when something is lost or missing. For example:\n我掉了钱包。 - I lost my wallet.\n他掉了手机。 - He lost his phone.\n\n## 3. Decrease\n\nIn certain contexts, "掉" can mean "decrease" when referring to a decline in quantity or quality. For example:\n销售额掉了20%。 - Sales have decreased by 20%.\n体重掉了五公斤。 - Weight has decreased by five kilograms.\n\n## 4. Shake off\n\nWhen used as a verb, "掉" can also mean "to shake off" or "to get rid of". For example:\n她想掉掉身上的灰尘。 - She wants to shake off the dust on her body.\n他试图掉掉不好的习惯。 - He is trying to get rid of bad habits.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "掉" can include "drop/fall", "lose", "decrease", and "shake off", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
