      'The Chinese character "告" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Tell, Inform, Notify\n\nThe most common translation of "告" is "tell", "inform", or "notify" when referring to communicating information to someone. For example:\n告诉他我们的计划。 - Tell him about our plan.\n警察告诉他要小心。 - The police informed him to be careful.\n\n## 2. Sue, Indict, Accuse\n\n"告" can also mean "sue", "indict", or "accuse" when referring to legal actions or accusations. For example:\n他告了他的竞争对手。 - He sued his competitor.\n她被告以煽动罪。 - She was indicted for incitement.\n\n## 3. Worship\n\nIn certain contexts, "告" can mean "worship" when referring to religious or ceremonial acts of reverence. For example:\n她在神庙里告了神。 - She worshipped the gods in the temple.\n\n## 4. Advertise\n\nWhen used as a verb, "告" can mean "to advertise" or "to publicize". For example:\n这则广告告诉人们新产品的优势。 - This ad advertises the advantages of the new product.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "告" can include "tell/inform/notify", "sue/indict/accuse", "worship", and "advertise", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
