      'The Italian word "Perché" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n1. **Because/Why**\n   - When "Perché" is used as a conjunction, it can be translated as "because" or "why" in English. For example:\n      - Ho mangiato molto perché avevo fame. - I ate a lot because I was hungry.\n      - Perché sei triste? - Why are you sad?\n\n2. **What/Why**\n   - "Perché" can also mean "what" or "why" in English when used as an interrogative pronoun. For example:\n      - Non capisco perché ha fatto così. - I don\'t understand why he/she did that.\n      - Perché non lo hai detto prima? - Why didn\'t you say it earlier?\n\n3. **Objective**\n   - In some cases, "Perché" can be translated as "objective" in English when used to express a purpose or goal. For example:\n      - Studiamo tanto perché possiamo superare l\'esame. - We study a lot so that we can pass the exam.\n      - Lavoro sodo perché possa permettermi una vacanza. - I work hard so that I can afford a vacation.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Italian word "Perché" can include "because/why", "what/why", and "objective", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
