    summary:
      'The Chinese character "累" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Tired, Exhausted\n\nThe most common translation of "累" is "tired" or "exhausted" when referring to a person feeling fatigue. For example:\n我今天工作很累。 - I am very tired from work today.\n他跑了一整天，现在很累了。 - He has been running all day and is very exhausted now.\n\n## 2. Accumulate, Increase\n\n"累" can also mean "to accumulate" or "to increase" when describing a gradual build-up or growth of something. For example:\n压力一点一点累积起来。 - The pressure gradually accumulates.\n努力工作会让你的财富累积。 - Hard work will help your wealth grow.\n\n## 3. Involved, Be Involved\n\nIn certain contexts, "累" can mean "to be involved" or "to have a connection" with something. For example:\n他被卷入了这场纷争之中，感觉很累。 - He got involved in the dispute and feels overwhelmed.\n\n## 4. Multiple, Repeated\n\nWhen used as a prefix or in combination with other characters, "累" can convey the idea of "multiple" or "repeated". For example:\n这是一起车祸，造成了多人受伤。 - This is a multiple-vehicle accident, resulting in multiple injuries.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "累" can include "tired/exhausted", "accumulate/increase", "involved/be involved", and "multiple/repeated", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
