    summary:
      'The Chinese character "活" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Live, Alive\n\nThe most common translation of "活" is "live" or "alive" when referring to living organisms or being in a state of life. For example:\n这只小猫很活泼。 - This kitten is very lively.\n他还活着。 - He is still alive.\n\n## 2. Activity, Action\n\n"活" can also mean "activity" or "action" when describing events or things in motion. For example:\n这个城市充满着生活的活力。 - This city is full of vibrant activity.\n她喜欢参加各种社交活动。 - She enjoys participating in various social activities.\n\n## 3. Flexible, Flexible\n\nIn certain contexts, "活" can mean "flexible" or "flexible" when describing something that can bend or move easily. For example:\n这个材料非常活。 - This material is very flexible.\n她是一位非常活的舞者。 - She is a very flexible dancer.\n\n## 4. Survive, Make a Living\n\nWhen used as a verb, "活" can mean "to survive" or "to make a living". For example:\n这些人靠捡垃圾活着。 - These people make a living by picking up garbage.\n他们设法在恶劣的环境中生存下来。 - They managed to survive in harsh conditions.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "活" can include "live/alive", "activity/action", "flexible", and "survive/make a living", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
