      'The Chinese character "靠" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Depend on, Lean on\n\nThe most common translation of "靠" is "depend on" or "lean on" when referring to relying on someone or something for support. For example:\n我可以靠你吗？ - Can I depend on you?\n他靠在墙上休息。 - He leaned on the wall to rest.\n\n## 2. By, Next to\n\n"靠" can also mean "by" or "next to" when describing proximity or position. For example:\n坐靠窗户的位置 - The seat by the window\n靠近学校 - Close to the school\n\n## 3. Touch, Against\n\nIn certain contexts, "靠" can mean "touch" or "against" when referring to physical contact. For example:\n门靠墙打开 - The door opens against the wall\n把椅子靠近桌子 - Move the chair closer to the table\n\n## 4. Rely on\n\nWhen used in a more abstract sense, "靠" can also be translated as "rely on" or "count on". For example:\n我们得靠自己解决这个问题。 - We have to rely on ourselves to solve this problem.\n靠着努力才能成功。 - Success depends on hard work.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "靠" can include "depend on/lean on", "by/next to", "touch/against", and "rely on/count on", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
