    summary:
      'The Chinese character "呆" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Silly, Dull\n\nThe most common translation of "呆" is "silly" or "dull" when describing someone as lacking in intelligence or being slow to react. For example:\n他看起来有点呆。 - He looks a bit silly.\n这孩子有时候有点呆。 - This child can be a bit dull sometimes.\n\n## 2. Stay, Stay still\n\n"呆" can also mean "to stay" or "to stay still" when referring to not moving or remaining in one place. For example:\n他呆在家里。 - He stays at home.\n她站在那里一动不动，呆呆地看着远方。 - She stood there motionless, staring into the distance.\n\n## 3. Stunned, Dazed\n\nIn certain contexts, "呆" can mean "stunned" or "dazed" when someone is in a state of shock or bewilderment. For example:\n他听到这个消息时呆住了。 - He was stunned when he heard the news.\n她站在那里，一脸呆滞。 - She stood there, looking dazed.\n\n## 4. Clueless, in a daze\n\n"呆" can also be used to describe someone as "clueless" or "in a daze" when they are lost or confused. For example:\n她完全呆住了，不知道该怎么办。 - She was completely clueless, not knowing what to do.\n看着地图一脸呆愣的模样，他们实在有点搞不清楚方向。 - Looking at the map with a clueless expression, they were a bit confused about the direction.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "呆" can include "silly/dull", "stay/stay still", "stunned/dazed", and "clueless/in a daze", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
