      'The Chinese character "却" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. But, However\n\nThe most common translation of "却" is "but" or "however" when used as a conjunction to indicate a contrast or contradiction. For example:\n他很努力，却并没有取得好成绩。 - He works very hard, but he did not achieve good results.\n她很忙，却还抽出时间帮助他人。 - She is very busy, however, she still finds time to help others.\n\n## 2. Instead\n\n"却" can also be translated as "instead" when indicating a replacement or alternative. For example:\n他原本打算去游泳，却改去跑步了。 - He originally planned to go swimming, but he went running instead.\n\n## 3. Leave, Go Away\n\nIn certain contexts, "却" can mean "leave" or "go away" as a verb. For example:\n他受不了这种态度，只好却到另一个地方去了。 - Unable to bear this attitude, he had to leave for another place.\n\n## 4. Prove, Verify\n\nAnother less common translation of "却" is "prove" or "verify" when used in specific situations. For example:\n这一事实却了他的猜想。 - This fact proved his conjecture.\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "却" can include "but/however", "instead", "leave/go away", and "prove/verify", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
