      'The Chinese character "确" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Sure, Certain\n\nThe most common translation of "确" is "sure" or "certain" when referring to being confident or having no doubt. For example:\n我确信他会成功。 - I am sure he will succeed.\n这个消息确凿无疑。 - This news is certain without a doubt.\n\n## 2. To Confirm, To Verify\n\n"确" can also mean "to confirm" or "to verify" when checking the accuracy or truth of something. For example:\n请您稍等，我需要确认一下。 - Please wait a moment, I need to confirm it.\n我们需要确保信息的准确性。 - We need to verify the accuracy of the information.\n\n## 3. Real, True\n\nIn certain contexts, "确" can mean "real" or "true" when emphasizing authenticity or genuineness. For example:\n这是真确的钻石。 - This is a real diamond.\n他对我的感情是确实的。 - His feelings towards me are true.\n\n## 4. To Ensure\n\nWhen used as a verb, "确" can mean "to ensure" or "to make sure" when emphasizing the importance of guaranteeing something. For example:\n请确保门锁好。 - Please ensure that the door is locked properly.\n我们必须确保食品安全。 - We must ensure food safety.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "确" can include "sure/certain", "to confirm/verify", "real/true", and "to ensure", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
