      'The Chinese character "双" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Pair, Double\n\nThe most common translation of "双" is "pair" or "double" when referring to a set of two things. For example:\n一双手套 - a pair of gloves\n双胞胎 - twins\n\n## 2. Both, Dual\n\n"双" can also mean "both" or "dual" when describing characteristics or actions involving two entities. For example:\n他们双双离开了。 - They both left.\n这家公司是双边合作的。 - This company is engaged in dual cooperation.\n\n## 3. To Agree\n\nIn certain contexts, "双" can also mean "to agree" or "to match". For example:\n他们双方都同意了这个决定。 - Both sides agreed to this decision.\n这两幅画双得很好。 - These two paintings match each other very well.\n\nSo in summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "双" can include "pair/double", "both/dual", and "to agree/match", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
