    summary:
      'The Chinese character "求" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Seek, Request\n\nOne common translation of "求" is "seek" or "request", especially when referring to asking for help, information, or a favor. For example:\n他在求助于警方。 - He is seeking help from the police.\n我求你别告诉任何人。 - I request that you don\'t tell anyone.\n\n## 2. Beg\n\n"求" can also mean "beg" when someone is pleading or begging for something. For example:\n她跪下来求他原谅。 - She knelt down to beg for his forgiveness.\n他一路求饶，但没有人肯放过他。 - He begged all the way, but no one would spare him.\n\n## 3. Pursue, Court\n\nIn certain contexts, "求" can mean "pursue" or "court", often used in a romantic or professional sense. For example:\n他在追求她已经很久了。 - He has been pursuing her for a long time.\n他通过不懈的努力求得了这个职位。 - He obtained this position through persistent efforts.\n\n## 4. Wish\n\nSometimes "求" can be translated as "wish" or "desire", expressing a hope or longing for something. For example:\n我求天愿，让我们的家庭幸福快乐。 - I wish for our family to be happy and joyful.\n他求之不得地赢得了比赛。 - He won the competition with the desire he had been longing for.\n\nIn conclusion, the English translations of the Chinese character "求" include "seek/request", "beg", "pursue/court", and "wish", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
