    summary:
      'The Chinese character "巨" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Huge, Gigantic\n\nThe most common translation of "巨" is "huge" or "gigantic" when referring to size or scale. For example:\n这只恐龙是巨大的。 - This dinosaur is huge.\n那座建筑的规模十分巨大。 - The scale of that building is gigantic.\n\n## 2. Giant\n\n"巨" can also mean "giant" when describing a person or creature of immense size. For example:\n传说中有一只巨人。 - There is a legend about a giant.\n这只巨蟒长得非常大。 - This giant python is very big.\n\n## 3. Enormous, Massive\n\nIn certain contexts, "巨" can convey the idea of something being "enormous" or "massive". For example:\n这次地震造成了巨大的破坏。 - This earthquake caused enormous damage.\n公司发布了一份巨额利润的财报。 - The company released a financial report showing massive profits.\n\n## 4. Mega\n\nWhen used informally or in modern contexts, "巨" can be translated as "mega" to emphasize something as being very large or significant. For example:\n这是一个巨型购物中心。 - This is a mega shopping center.\n他们达成了一项巨额合同。 - They secured a mega contract.\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "巨" can include "huge/gigantic", "giant", "enormous/massive", and "mega", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
