    summary:
      'The Chinese character "受" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Receive, Accept\n\nOne common translation of "受" is "receive" or "accept" when referring to receiving something physically or emotionally. For example:\n我受到了他的帮助。 - I received his help.\n她受不了这么多压力。 - She can\'t accept so much pressure.\n\n## 2. Undergo, Experience\n\n"受" can also mean "undergo" or "experience" when describing going through a certain situation or feeling. For example:\n他受了很大的折磨。 - He underwent a lot of torture.\n这次考试很受挫。 - This exam was very discouraging.\n\n## 3. Bear, Suffer\n\nIn certain contexts, "受" can mean "bear" or "suffer" when talking about enduring hardship or pain. For example:\n他受了很重的伤。 - He suffered a serious injury.\n这种疾病很难受。 - This illness is very hard to bear.\n\n## 4. Admit, Allow\n\nSometimes, "受" can also be translated as "admit" or "allow" when discussing permission or acknowledgment. For example:\n他不受任何限制。 - He doesn\'t allow any restrictions.\n她受到了表扬。 - She received praise.\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "受" can include "receive/accept", "undergo/experience", "bear/suffer", and "admit/allow", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
