    summary:
      'The Chinese character "来" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Come\n\nThe most common translation of "来" is "come" when referring to someone\'s movement towards the speaker or a particular location. For example:\n他来了。 - He has come.\n我想去中国旅行，你来吗？ - I want to travel to China, will you come?\n\n## 2. Next\n\n"来" can also mean "next" when indicating the order or sequence of something. For example:\n下一个来的人请问。 - The next person, please come.\n下周来办公室开会。 - Come to the office for a meeting next week.\n\n## 3. Implies the future\n\nIn certain contexts, "来" can imply the future or upcoming time. For example:\n今年会有很多挑战来临。 - Many challenges will come this year.\n等着明天来吧。 - Wait for tomorrow to come.\n\n## 4. Particles\n\n"来" can also be used as a particle in certain expressions to indicate completion or suggestion. For example:\n吃饭来了吧。 - Let\'s eat (suggesting completion of a task).\n谈完了来继续我们的计划。 - Let\'s continue our plan after finishing the discussion.\n\n\nIn summary, the English translations of the Chinese character "来" can include "come", "next", implications of the future, and as a particle in various expressions, depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
