    summary:
      'The Japanese character "を" (pronounced as "wo" in romaji) is a unique character known as a particle (助詞/joshi) in the Japanese language. It is primarily used as a marker for the direct object of a verb in a sentence. Although it is written as "wo," it is pronounced as "o" in modern Japanese. Here are a few key points about the character "を":\n\n## Direct Object Marker\n\nを is placed after the object noun in a sentence to indicate that the action of the verb is being done to that object. For example:\nマギーさんがリンゴを食べます。- Maggie eats an apple.\n\n## Pronunciation\n\nWhile を is written as "wo," it is pronounced as "o" in modern Japanese. This discrepancy between spelling and pronunciation is a unique feature of this character.\n\n## Usage\n\nを is a grammatical marker that is crucial for indicating the direct object in a sentence. It is always placed directly after the object noun and before the verb.\n\nIn summary, を is a Japanese particle used to mark the direct object in a sentence, despite being written as "wo," it is pronounced as "o" in modern Japanese.',
