      'The Chinese character "还" has several translations in English, depending on the context:\n\n## 1. Still, Yet\n\nOne common translation of "还" is "still" or "yet" when expressing actions or states that are ongoing or have not changed. For example:\n他还在睡觉。 - He is still sleeping.\n我还没吃早饭。 - I haven\'t eaten breakfast yet.\n\n## 2. Also, Too\n\n"还" can also mean "also" or "too" when adding additional information or indicating similarity. For example:\n我喜欢吃水果，还喜欢吃蔬菜。 - I like to eat fruits, and I also like to eat vegetables.\n他会说英语，还会说法语。 - He can speak English and he can also speak French.\n\n## 3. Return, Give Back\n\nIn another context, "还" can be translated as "return" or "give back" when referring to actions involving returning something. For example:\n请还我钱。 - Please give me back the money.\n还书 - Return the book\n\n## 4. In addition, Yet\n\n"还" can also mean "in addition" or "yet" when presenting additional information or points. For example:\n他很聪明，还很勤奋。 - He is very smart, yet he is also hardworking.\n这本书很有趣，还很有教育意义。 - This book is very interesting, and yet it is also very educational.\n\nOverall, the English translations of the Chinese character "还" can include "still/yet", "also/too", "return/give back", and "in addition/yet", depending on the specific context in which it is used.',
